faq:
  - q: "How do you answer 'what are your strengths' in an interview?"
    a: "Name one specific, relevant strength, explain it with evidence, and connect it to the role. Example: 'My strongest skill is architectural thinking — I can hold system complexity in my head and make tradeoffs explicit earlier than most engineers. At [Company], this showed up most clearly when I caught a single-point-of-failure risk in our payment processing service before we shipped it. We redesigned the fault isolation layer in two days and avoided what would have been a major incident.' Do not say 'I am a hard worker' or 'I am very passionate.' These are not strengths — they are filler."
  - q: "How do you answer 'what are your weaknesses' in an interview?"
    a: "Give a real weakness — not a fake one disguised as a strength. 'I work too hard' and 'I'm a perfectionist' are recognized as evasion immediately. The key is to pair the real weakness with a specific, concrete coping strategy. Example: 'I have historically underestimated how long written communication takes me. I draft things quickly but revise a lot. I have compensated by time-boxing my drafting — 20 minutes for a first draft, one revision — and my throughput has improved significantly.'"
  - q: "Can you say 'I'm a perfectionist' as a weakness?"
    a: "No. It is the most overused fake weakness in interviews. Interviewers have heard it hundreds of times and it actively signals that you are not self-aware enough to name a real growth area. It reads as evasive and condescending. Give a real weakness with a real mitigation strategy."
  - q: "How many strengths should you mention?"
    a: "One to two, in depth. Many candidates try to list five strengths quickly to sound impressive. Depth beats breadth. A single, specific, well-evidenced strength leaves a stronger impression than five generic ones. If asked for more than one, give a second at a different level — technical and interpersonal, for example."
  - q: "What do interviewers evaluate with strengths and weaknesses questions?"
    a: "Three things: (1) Self-awareness — do you have an accurate read on yourself? (2) Relevance — is your strength aligned with what this role needs? (3) Maturity — can you discuss your weaknesses honestly without becoming defensive or giving a non-answer? Candidates who cannot name a real weakness signal low self-awareness, which is a red flag for senior roles."
